WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.720
It's being described as Spain's most divisive
general election in decades.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.470
Voters head to the polls again today - for
the third time in four years.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:14.650
The party of Socialist Prime Minister Pedro
Sanchez is expected to get the most votes

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.850
-- but may not garner enough support to form
a government.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:24.130
That means he'll need the backing of smaller
parties, which are enjoying a surge in support.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.300
Many voters are still weighing their options.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.949
There's still plenty of indecision on the
streets of Madrid.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:35.960
Polls show about four in ten Spaniards don't
know who to vote for on Sunday.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.920
For many, it's no longer simply a choice between
the two traditional parties - the center-left

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.940
Spanish Socialist Workers' Party, or the center-right
People's Party.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.600
"I am going to vote for the left wing party
Unidas Podemos, because the traditional parties

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.969
have deceived us too often.

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:56.600
They were involved in so many corruption scandals
and have never delivered on their promises."

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.130
"I think it's great that there are now more
than only two alternatives.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:06.939
I couldn't identify with any of the traditional
parties but there weren't any other real options."

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:13.689
The far-right Vox party looks set to win seats
in the national parliament for the first time.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:18.609
It's predicted to win at least ten percent
of the vote with its populist, anti-immigrant

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:24.929
and anti-feminist message - a sentiment that
resonates with many.

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:32.279
"I like how they defend Spain and our civil
liberties, they want to cut taxes and put

00:01:32.279 --> 00:01:39.270
an end to the gender ideology that promotes
abortion and criminalizes men.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.340
We young people are sick of our traditional
politicians, doing the opposite of what they

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.340
promise."

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.560
40 years after the end of General Franco's
dictatorship, Spain finds itself with a resurgent

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.149
far-right - just like many of its European
neighbors.

00:01:55.149 --> 00:02:23.269
"Spain’s traditional

00:02:23.269 --> 00:03:14.749
parties will no longer be able to form a 
government on their own.

00:03:14.749 --> 00:03:58.430
So it’s the small parties that will determine
who will run this country for the coming four

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:06.079
years."

